    "note": "The readers' vocabulary predates the 4.1 amendment and has no NEUTRAL_ACK. On all 30 such spans reader A said OFF_TARGET and reader B said WITHHELD_ASSERTED - unanimously each, and unanimously NOT WITHHELD_UNCERTAIN. That is independent blind confirmation that the amendment was right and the pre-amendment label was wrong; it is NOT evidence the taxonomy agrees with humans.",
    "reader_A_label": "OFF_TARGET",
    "reader_B_label": "WITHHELD_ASSERTED"
  },
  "finding": "The deepest number here is the INTER-READER agreement, 0.733. Two careful blind readers agree with each other on fewer than three quarters of the spans, so no rule can exceed that against them: the construct 'exactly one MECE label per span' is not reliably human-decidable at this granularity. The failure is in the task definition, not only in the rule. Consequence for the prior work: docs/drafts/TAXONOMY_withholding.md's 94 hand-adjudicated labels rest on ONE reader with no agreement statistic, and this experiment gives the first estimate of what a second reader would have done - disagree about a quarter of the time.",
